WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:05.170
LEE BOONSTRA: Hi, guys.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.620
Whoop.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:07.370
Welcome.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:08.540
My name is Lee Boonstra.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.370
I work as a customer
engineer for Google.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.720
And that means that I help
in the pre-sales process,

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.560
customers, with creating
prototypes or answering

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.420
questions about all
our Google products.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.320
And I have a background
in software development.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.020
So Google asked
me like, hey, Lee,

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:30.770
do you want to give a talk
about G Suite this year?

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:34.610
I said, yes, but I like
to give a technical talk.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.430
I love to talk about how you
can extend on top of G Suite,

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.220
whether it's in your
own applications

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:44.360
or whether you want to write
code on top of G Suite,

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.550
because that is
basically the idea on how

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:49.910
if you want to extend on
it, there are two ways.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.100
You can take the
Apps Script approach.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.680
That means, like, I stay
within the Google domain

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:56.990
but in a G Suite domain.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:01.610
And I write my own
code on top of G Suite.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.620
You can do that
through Apps Script.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.380
The other approach, that
is through Google APIs.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.540
That is, I have my
own application,

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.280
and I host it somewhere.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.010
But I want to bring my
data inside of my apps.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:15.260
I want to get everything
from G Suite inside

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.880
of my own applications, so to
leverage the power of G Suite.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.940
So you probably would take
one of these approaches.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.680
I'm curious how many developers
are here in the room?

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:27.480
A few?

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.540
Or how many people
read code once before?

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.580
Yes, a little bit more, right?

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.350
For the people
that are not really

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:42.090
into coding but they have great
ideas on building applications,

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.580
I will also cover
apps, App Maker--

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.200
that is our visual
design editor--

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.010
how you quickly can create
applications, basically,

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.130
by dragging and dropping,
so with no code.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.750
And that's going to be
interesting as well.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:58.330
But let's talk about
Apps Script first.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.250
What is Apps Script?

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.490
Apps Script, it's
basically JavaScript,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:05.157
but it runs on a
server, serverless.

00:02:05.157 --> 00:02:06.990
That doesn't mean that
there are no servers.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:08.820
No, there are servers
arranged somewhere.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.800
But Google all
takes care of that.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.540
And we just write JavaScript
code as if it was in a browser.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.290
But just keep in mind, it runs
somewhere on a Google server.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:22.740
And together with that, it
also has all these nice APIs,

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.630
these Google APIs,
and services that

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:30.560
are all available within
your Apps Script environment.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.600
And with that, you can create
your own add-ons or extensions

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.480
or themes, and the
big advantage here

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.850
is that once you do that
through Apps Script,

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.750
you no longer need
to deal with OAuth.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.309
That has all been
taken care of you.

00:02:43.309 --> 00:02:44.850
[? In ?] OAuth, that
is a way how you

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.880
can secure your applications.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.190
The developers here
probably will understand,

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.000
like to set that up,
that could be difficult.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.350
But this has all been arranged
in the environment from G

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:56.850
Suite.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.270
So that is nice.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.780
And with that, you also
get a lot of other tools,

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.630
like monitoring
tools and logging.

00:03:06.587 --> 00:03:08.420
And then once you have
your add-on finished,

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:13.010
you can drop it in a marketplace
or in the Chrome Web Store,

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.155
or you can have it
internally being shared.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.424
There are, in general, four
different entry points.

00:03:21.424 --> 00:03:23.840
And if you want to write Apps
Script code, where would you

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:24.710
start?

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:27.090
And I think the most common
one that will be like,

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.030
I have in a document
or sheet and I

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.170
want to write scripts for
that particular document

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:34.070
or particular sheet.

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.050
So there is like a
relation between your code

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.270
and that sheet.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.000
We call that
container-bound script.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.480
So it's one document
or one sheet,

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.250
together bundled with your code.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.490
And to talk to that code,
what you would first

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:51.770
do in your code, you would
say, like, documentApp

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:55.610
and then getActiveDocument
or getActiveSpreadsheet.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:59.370
So you have a reference to that
active tab or active sheet.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.182
And then you can
start coding on it.

00:04:01.182 --> 00:04:02.390
You will write some triggers.

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:05.510
You say, like, every time
when I open this document,

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.880
I execute this piece
of code, or every time

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:13.440
I edit this document, I run some
secret code in the background.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.649
That's how you have
to set that up.

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:18.709
Now, the other
entry point it will

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:22.250
be like it's not bound to a
particular document or sheet.

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:24.357
In this case, you
would create like you

00:04:24.357 --> 00:04:25.940
would create a normal
web application,

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.649
but with Apps Script.

00:04:27.649 --> 00:04:29.190
So you would go to
script.google.com,

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:33.560
or you go to your Google Drive.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:39.710
And from there, you open the
Apps Script native editor.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:41.940
And then you start coding
your scripts over there.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.570
And typically what people do
is, then, when you execute,

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.250
when you run such
a piece of code,

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:47.760
then they show a file picker.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.110
And the user can select
the sheet or document

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:55.524
that they need, and then you
can start executing the code.

00:04:55.524 --> 00:04:56.690
That way it's more reusable.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:04.790
Now, there's another entry
point that is more like AdWords.

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:08.010
It's like in case you're
doing stuff with advertising.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.680
You can also code App Script
code, so JavaScript code which

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.700
runs on the server, but
it's particular for AdWord.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.430
And the last one, although
App Maker, in general, you

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.300
don't necessarily need to code,
you could code if you want.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.970
And there you have the choice
between normal JavaScript

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:25.590
code or also Apps Script.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:28.490
And then again, it
runs on the server.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:31.920
We will talk about this later.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.630
So how would I write
Apps Script code?

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:36.840
I'll show you, like, a simple
demo on how you could do that.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.020
But first, as a
developer, what I would do

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:40.740
is I would read the manual.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.470
So I go to the docs
page from Google.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.650
There they really
explain it pretty well,

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:49.160
like, all the APIs
methods that are available

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:50.540
and that you can start using.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:52.340
If it's for a
container-bound script,

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:54.350
or if it's for your
own web applications,

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:55.504
it's all available there.

00:05:55.504 --> 00:05:57.170
And that's definitely
the starting point

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:58.086
where you would start.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:02.960
In this case, I went
to the Google Drive.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.159
I start opening my native
editor, Google Apps Script.

00:06:06.159 --> 00:06:07.700
I could also
[INAUDIBLE] @google.com.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.670
And what I'm going to
create in this example

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:14.720
is a kind of conference
application, where speakers

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:16.430
can upload their presentation.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.560
And every time when a
presentation was uploaded,

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:23.090
I will get an email to ask me if
I can approve the presentations

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:24.500
and can read through it.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:26.730
I think this is a very
simple application,

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:30.080
but a use case that I
think everybody could,

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.087
yeah, could create.

00:06:33.087 --> 00:06:34.670
So what you would
do here in this case

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:36.790
is you would go to
their Google Drive.

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:37.964
You will create a folder.

00:06:37.964 --> 00:06:39.380
And you need to
share that folder,

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:41.750
because then all the
other speakers can upload

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:43.560
their presentation in there.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:46.220
And when you do
that, you get an ID.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:49.010
And that is an ID
that you need to copy,

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:54.410
because this is the idea we
will refer to in our code.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:57.170
So in our native editor,
we will create a variable

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:59.080
called Slides Folder ID.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:02.420
And then I paste the code
that I just copied in there.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:06.440
And then I start
writing JavaScript code.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:07.520
It's very easy.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.680
In this case, I create
a variable folder,

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.340
and I say, like, drive
application, get folder by ID.

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:16.550
And I take the ID
that just copied.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:19.130
The next thing is I want to
retrieve all the files that

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:20.340
are in that folder.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:23.990
So I can do that
with folder.getFiles.

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:26.630
And the next thing on line
6, what I'm doing here

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.570
is I create a kind of store.

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:31.340
So what I want to do
is I want to create

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:34.790
some kind of persistence
layer here, so simple storage

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:39.470
that every time, next week,
if I open this script,

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:42.230
that it still has some way of
storing which files are new

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:44.720
and which files are old.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.780
So it's a kind of
key value store.

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:49.880
It's very simple.

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:51.720
So that's what
I'm creating here.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.500
The next thing is I create
a variable, new files.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:57.320
This is just for flagging
if the file is new.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:00.230
And then I will loop through
all the files, the file iterator

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:02.390
that I created before.

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:04.790
And I check if the file is new.

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:08.270
And if the file is new,
then I'm going to add it

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.400
to the new files array.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:12.830
So then I can do
something with that later.

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:15.050
And I will also add it
to the persistence layer

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:18.410
so that next week when I run
this script, then the new file

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.930
won't show up again.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:23.340
And the last piece of
the code, in this case,

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.610
I check, like, oh, are
there any new files?

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:26.780
Well, yes there are.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:28.500
OK, I'm going to lock them.

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:30.560
I'm using basic locking here.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.720
And I'll send an email.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.360
And the email, that's done
through the email API.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:38.659
So I can say gmailapp.sendEmail.

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:40.370
What am I sending?

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:43.490
I sent an email to the
active user, which is me.

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:47.690
And I retrieve file
getName and file getURL.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:50.780
So that is what I
push in the email.

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:52.650
And then I can start testing it.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:55.850
So I would press the
Play button here.

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:59.450
At that moment, I will
probably see a log message.

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:03.230
So you can go to your basic
logging, so view logs.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:04.790
And then you would
see a log message

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:07.072
like what you see
on the top screen.

00:09:07.072 --> 00:09:08.780
But if you want to
have it more advanced,

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:11.270
it is possible to
use Stackdriver here.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:13.580
And then you would
use console.log.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:15.450
And then once it
is in Stackdriver,

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:19.690
you have much nicer, richer
stack traces and better

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:20.190
logging.

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.020
So that's an option for you.

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:27.650
This is the email, how
it would look like when

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:28.640
I retrieve that email.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.910
Now, that was just a test.

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:34.920
How can I bring
that to production?

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:38.310
Well, I need to set up some
project triggers in this case.

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.430
That means, like, I'm
going to schedule it,

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.740
that every time it will run.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:47.340
When I create my first onProject
triggers there aren't any.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.430
So I need to go
through this process.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:52.770
This also means that I
need to be authenticated.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.720
I need to authorize
myself that I

00:09:54.720 --> 00:10:02.105
am allowed to open the drive
box and also the email.

00:10:02.105 --> 00:10:03.480
So I [INAUDIBLE]
the permissions.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:06.230
You get like a nice
all-out pop-up.

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:07.890
And you allow that pop up.

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:11.520
And you can see also I need
read, send, and deleted access

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:12.780
for Gmail.

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:15.480
That doesn't necessarily need
to be the case, because I'm only

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:16.410
sending an email.

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:18.620
But definitely for if
you want to read email

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:22.380
from your Gmail account, then
you need to approve on that.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:27.590
And I also need to give
access to do the drive.

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:28.920
I then accept the trigger.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:32.640
And in this case, I create a
time trigger for five minutes.

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.229
And every five minutes it
starts running the script.

00:10:35.229 --> 00:10:37.020
And then it sees if
there are new files in,

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:39.660
and sends me an email
if there are any.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:43.620
This all runs on
the Google server.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:47.970
There are a couple of things
on the roadmap for Apps Script

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:49.320
and add-ons in general.

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:51.120
And one of these things
is Gmail add-ons,

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.790
which is something I like
because now we can probably

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:56.280
create kind of
templates in Gmail

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:59.390
that you can automatically
send reports or so.

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:02.520
Slides will also
get add-on feature.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:05.030
I use a lot of slides, as I do
a lot of these kind of talks.

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:06.700
So I'm very happy with that.

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:08.740
That is actually an
early access program.

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:10.330
So if you want, you
can try this out.

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:12.270
Just register for that.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:14.940
And also mobile
add-ons, so if you

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:19.590
create Apps Script
for docs or sheets,

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:21.660
then it will also work
on your mobile phone.

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:24.120
That works through your
mobile phone via the browser

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:26.330
but on native also on Android.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:33.720
Now let's take a look into
the second approach that

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.010
will be the Google APIs.

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:41.890
Google supports many, many
languages, if not all,

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:44.050
almost all of the popular ones.

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:49.410
Think about node.js or
JavaScript, Java.net, Ruby,

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:52.290
also for mobile, Go language.

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:55.360
Google has all client SDKs
for all these languages.

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.810
And you can just start
downloading such an SDK

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:01.226
and then implement that in
your own web application.

00:12:01.226 --> 00:12:02.850
So in this case, what
we're going to do

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:08.560
is we're going to integrate G
Suite in our own applications.

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:10.990
Through such an API,
such as client SDK,

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:12.760
you get all the end points.

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:16.390
And with that you can retrieve
access to the Google Drive

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:19.360
or to your sheets.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:21.460
So let me show you
an example of that.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:25.210
I created a simple application
myself the other week,

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:27.280
where I wanted to create--

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:29.140
for my Google Home,
I wanted to make sure

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.690
that the Google Home would
read up all my emails.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:35.170
And the way how I wanted to do
that is by putting all my email

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:38.290
headers in a sheet and
then asked Google Home

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:41.200
to read up the sheet.

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:42.880
So I created that
with node.js because I

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:44.380
am a JavaScript developer.

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:46.620
So I went to NPM.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:48.910
I downloaded the package.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:52.930
The next thing is in my Google
Cloud, I go to Gmail API,

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:55.030
and I need to enable APIs.

00:12:55.030 --> 00:12:56.762
So every API that
you want to use,

00:12:56.762 --> 00:12:58.720
you need to make sure
that you first enable it.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:00.760
And at that moment,
you can also monitor it

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:04.480
and see the amount of users that
has been used through your API.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:06.860
And you can secure
it, so not secretly.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:08.710
Other people can
start using the API.

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:13.120
So it's really all secure within
the-- yeah, with the OAuth

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.820
from Google.

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:18.100
You set up your credentials.

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:24.160
What I did is I created a JSON
file, a JSON credentials key.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:25.990
Service account we call that.

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:29.400
And I put that in
my folder structure.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:32.380
And a service account
contains all the information

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:34.330
of connecting to my account.

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:38.860
I need to set that up
and whitelist that.

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:43.720
Then I need to start
looking into OAuth2.

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:46.200
That can be tricky,
but luckily the SDKs,

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:47.510
they have support for that.

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:52.880
So you can start using the
predefined code from Google.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:54.760
And that is what I
did in this case.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:57.290
First, I generated the URL.

00:13:57.290 --> 00:13:59.710
The SDK did that for me.

00:13:59.710 --> 00:14:03.370
With that URL, I can log
into my own Google account.

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:05.440
So at that moment, it
knows that it's about me

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:07.720
and that I give access to
certain files or folders.

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:13.620
I get redirected to a different
page, and I get a new code.

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:18.640
And with that code I create an
[? alternate ?] object, which

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:20.170
will be a [INAUDIBLE] token.

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:22.804
And what I did is I saved
that in my folder structure

00:14:22.804 --> 00:14:24.220
so that the next
time I don't need

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:26.860
to go through this whole process
again when I'm on localhost.

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:34.770
Now then the code, code is
not so difficult either.

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:36.660
I started with
implementing the packages.

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:39.390
So I say, I require
the Google APIs

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:41.650
and I require the Google
[INAUDIBLE] library.

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:44.130
So I don't need to do all
the OAuth stuff myself.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:46.020
I can just copy-paste
that, which

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.280
is to authorize the get
new token and the store

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.970
token functions.

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:52.570
I need to define scopes.

00:14:52.570 --> 00:14:55.890
Now, in this case, I want
to get access to Gmail.

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:57.060
So I set the scope for that.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:59.340
And I also created
a folder where

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:02.430
I-- or I point to the folder
where I saved the service

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:04.150
token on my hard drive.

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:06.390
So then node.js can
figure that out.

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:12.660
And this is the actual
code that I wrote myself.

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:14.520
By looking into
the documentations,

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:18.060
I said gmail.users
messages list.

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:21.690
So I want to retrieve all the
emails that are in my inbox.

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:24.930
And in this case,
I query on unread.

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:28.619
So these are new messages that
are coming in in my email box.

00:15:28.619 --> 00:15:30.660
If it went wrong, because
this is a callback what

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:32.830
you see here, if
it went wrong, it

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:36.150
shows me an error
message in Stackdriver.

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:39.030
If it went fine, then
it gets into here.

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:42.540
And then I save the
emails in variable emails.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:45.050
This will be an array with IDs.

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:47.040
You probably choose
such an ID want

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:51.330
to retrieve the body of
the email and the subject.

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:54.540
So then you would say
gmail.users.message.get.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:57.569
And then you query on the
ID that you just retrieved.

00:15:57.569 --> 00:15:59.610
And at that moment, then
you have all the emails.

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.680
Then I would put it in my--

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:04.440
I would make another call
to access my spreadsheet

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:07.530
and save it in there.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:09.380
Now, there are
many APIs like this

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:11.600
that you can start
using for Google Drive,

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:15.980
for Calendar,
Gmail, you name it.

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:19.400
Almost every Google
project has an own API

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:23.204
that you can start using
and playing around with.

00:16:23.204 --> 00:16:24.620
Definitely, if
you're a developer,

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:25.953
I would definitely look into it.

00:16:25.953 --> 00:16:28.340
Because Google made so
much nice things for you,

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:31.760
that you can very
easily just start using.

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:34.650
So pretty awesome.

